.TH MAKE2COMPDB 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
make2compdb \- generate a compilation database from make output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B make2compdb
.RB [ \-v ]
.br
.B make2compdb
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B make2compdb
reads the commands printed by
.BR make (1)
on standard input and writes a JSON compilation database, the
.I compile_commands.json
used by clangd and other tools, on standard output.
Run
.B make
with
.B \-B
so that it prints every command even when its targets are up to date,
.B \-w
so that it reports each directory it enters and leaves, and
.B \-n
so that it prints the commands without running them.
.PP
Each input line is either a directory message from
.BR make ,
or shell commands.
A directory message is a line such as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
make[1]: Entering directory \(aqC:/src/lib\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
whose first word ends in a colon, and its matching
.B Leaving directory
line.
These maintain a stack of directories, which starts with the current
working directory.
.PP
Shell commands are split into words as by a POSIX shell, honouring
quotes, backslash escapes, and line continuations.
Control operators such as
.BR ; ,
.BR && ,
and
.B |
separate commands.
In each command,
.B make2compdb
looks for the first word that names a C or C++ compiler, and drops the
words before it, such as
.B ccache
or variable assignments.
It also drops redirections with their targets, command substitutions,
and
.B ${...}
expansions.
.PP
A compiler is recognized by its name, ignoring any directory and
.B .exe
suffix:
.BR gcc ,
.BR g++ ,
.BR clang ,
.BR clang++ ,
.BR cc ,
and
.BR c++ ,
also with a target prefix such as
.BR x86_64\-w64\-mingw32\-gcc ,
a version suffix such as
.BR gcc\-15 ,
or the
.B \-win32
and
.B \-posix
suffixes of Debian's Mingw-w64 GCC; and
.BR zig ,
.BR icx ,
.BR icpx ,
.BR nvc ,
.BR nvc++ ,
.BR tcc ,
.BR emcc ,
and
.BR armclang .
MSVC
.B cl
and
.B clang\-cl
are recognized but not supported, and produce no entries.
.PP
The source files of a command are its arguments ending in
.BR .c ,
.BR .cc ,
.BR .cpp ,
.BR .cxx ,
.BR .c++ ,
.BR .C ,
.BR .cppm ,
.BR .s ,
or
.BR .S .
After an
.B \-x
option naming a C, C++, or assembler language, every non-option argument
is a source file, until
.BR "\-x none" .
After any other language, such as
.BR "\-x c\-header" ,
none are.
A command with no source files, such as a link step, produces no
entries.
.PP
Each source file produces one entry, with these members:
.TP
.B directory
The directory on top of the stack.
.TP
.B file
The source file as written in the command.
.TP
.B output
The argument of
.B \-o
or
.BR \-\-output ,
when present.
.TP
.B arguments
The words of the command, from the compiler on.
When a command compiles several source files, each entry omits the
others.
.PP
Backslashes in Windows paths, including the directories, become forward
slashes.
If standard input is a console,
.B make2compdb
reads nothing and writes an empty array.
.SH OPTIONS
Each option must be a separate argument.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Describe the parsing of each line on standard error: its words, the
compiler found, and the entries produced.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Unknown argument, or an error writing output.
.SH EXAMPLES
Generate a database for the project in the current directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ make \-Bwn | make2compdb >compile_commands.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Generate an entry for a single command:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo \(aqccache gcc \-c \-o main.o main.c\(aq | make2compdb
[
    {
        "directory": "C:/src/demo",
        "file": "main.c",
        "output": "main.o",
        "arguments": [
            "gcc",
            "\-c",
            "\-o",
            "main.o",
            "main.c"
        ]
    }
]
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B make \-n
still runs recursive
.B $(MAKE)
commands, which is how the commands of sub-makes reach the output.
.B make2compdb
sees only the commands as printed.
It does not look inside scripts that the build runs, nor expand response
files.
.PP
The whole input and output must fit in 64 MiB of memory.
Beyond that,
.B make2compdb
terminates abnormally.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gcc (1),
.BR make (1)
